WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:21.842 --> 00:00:23.550
STEPHANIE WONG: Hey,
everyone and welcome

00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:27.120
to the very first episode
of Ask Google Cloud.

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:29.400
The show where you get to
ask Google Cloud experts

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:32.910
your burning questions like,
are dogs or cats better?

00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:34.410
I'm just guessing
that's going to be

00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:35.970
way too controversial
so we're just

00:00:35.970 --> 00:00:38.230
going to stick to Cloud topics.

00:00:38.230 --> 00:00:40.950
I'm Stephanie Wong, a Google
Cloud developer advocate.

00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:44.370
And today I'm joined by Martin
Omander, developer advocate

00:00:44.370 --> 00:00:48.390
for Google Cloud Serverless,
and James Ward, also

00:00:48.390 --> 00:00:51.037
developer advocate, also
focused on Serverless.

00:00:51.037 --> 00:00:53.370
So today we're going to be
answering questions submitted

00:00:53.370 --> 00:00:58.170
by you, our amazing and dynamic
Google Cloud YouTube audience.

00:00:58.170 --> 00:01:00.360
We're always looking
out for your questions

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:03.600
about Google Cloud products, so
be sure to leave any questions

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:06.180
you might have in the
comments of our videos

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:09.210
using the hashtag
#AskGoogleCloud.

00:01:09.210 --> 00:01:10.588
We actually read them.

00:01:10.588 --> 00:01:11.130
Yeah, I know.

00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:12.510
What a concept, right?

00:01:12.510 --> 00:01:14.940
And if you also have other
questions that come up

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:18.100
as you're watching today, please
type them in the live chat

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:21.690
and we will do our best to
try and get to them all.

00:01:21.690 --> 00:01:23.850
We'll be hosting Ask
Google Cloud segments

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:26.520
throughout the year, so be sure
to subscribe to the channel

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:30.240
to get notified every time
a new episode comes out.

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:32.940
With that said, let's go ahead
and get started with today's

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:35.430
topic, "All Things Serverless."

00:01:35.430 --> 00:01:39.990
So our first question
comes from @Ajayanudeep.

00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:41.820
Sorry if I butcher names.

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:44.970
What workloads work
well for Serverless?

00:01:44.970 --> 00:01:47.940
I think this might be the
million question of today.

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:51.390
So Martin, why don't we go
ahead and start with you?

00:01:51.390 --> 00:01:55.290
MARTIN OMANDER: Yeah, we do a
lot of surveys with developers

00:01:55.290 --> 00:01:57.240
and ask them what they
use Serverless for.

00:01:57.240 --> 00:02:01.440
And one of the top use cases
that always comes in is APIs.

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:04.050
Either you have an API that
you build between your system

00:02:04.050 --> 00:02:05.280
and another system.

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:08.190
Or you have an API that
sits between your backend

00:02:08.190 --> 00:02:12.660
and your mobile client, or your
backend and your web client.

00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:14.670
And Serverless is great
for that because we're

00:02:14.670 --> 00:02:17.880
talking about API calls that's
like a quick little thing that

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:18.690
needs to run.

00:02:18.690 --> 00:02:21.170
And load can vary a
lot over time as well.

00:02:21.170 --> 00:02:22.860
And Serverless really
shines for that.

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:24.630
STEPHANIE WONG: Amazing
explanation, yeah.

00:02:24.630 --> 00:02:28.380
JAMES WARD: Yeah, anything
with real dynamic load

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:31.300
where it's constantly
changing the traffic.

00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:34.140
So if it's fluctuating
based on time of day

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:37.560
or maybe you need to
support a Super Bowl ad,

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:40.380
something of that scale,
the ability to scale from

00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:43.500
zero all of a sudden
to millions of people

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:44.970
using a particular service.

00:02:44.970 --> 00:02:46.740
That's where
Serverless is great.

00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:48.817
It's just that huge
dynamic fluctuating scale.

00:02:48.817 --> 00:02:50.400
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah,
it sounds like--

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:52.270
JAMES WARD: Data processing
is another great one.

00:02:52.270 --> 00:02:53.645
STEPHANIE WONG:
It sounds like it

00:02:53.645 --> 00:02:56.850
can vary from small workloads
to large, so is there a size

00:02:56.850 --> 00:02:58.440
differential or
is there something

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:01.020
that it's more particularly
suited towards in terms

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:04.110
of workload size or API calls?

00:03:04.110 --> 00:03:06.900
MARTIN OMANDER: I think
it actually works really

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:11.460
well for small workloads when
you have something every now

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:12.990
and then that needs to run.

00:03:12.990 --> 00:03:15.540
Then you don't have to pay
for the server 24/7, only

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:16.380
when it's executing.

00:03:16.380 --> 00:03:18.480
But also good for
large workloads

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:20.260
like you were
saying, James, here.

00:03:20.260 --> 00:03:23.620
The Super Bowl ad comes on
or Black Friday rolls around

00:03:23.620 --> 00:03:27.480
and it's a big
variation in traffic

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:30.630
when this large
traffic-- so I think it's

00:03:30.630 --> 00:03:31.910
actually good for both.

00:03:31.910 --> 00:03:32.910
STEPHANIE WONG: Awesome.

00:03:32.910 --> 00:03:34.350
OK, so we have a
lot of questions

00:03:34.350 --> 00:03:36.510
so our second
question comes from,

00:03:36.510 --> 00:03:39.510
@Jayesh, who asks,
"How are serverless

00:03:39.510 --> 00:03:41.285
architectures better?"

00:03:41.285 --> 00:03:42.660
And I feel like
this might be one

00:03:42.660 --> 00:03:45.030
of those it depends
answers but let's go ahead

00:03:45.030 --> 00:03:47.283
and go to you, James.

00:03:47.283 --> 00:03:48.200
JAMES WARD: Sure, yes.

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:50.690
I think to talk about this
one of the useful things

00:03:50.690 --> 00:03:53.810
to focus on is Cloud
Native architectures.

00:03:53.810 --> 00:03:56.210
And when you build
for Cloud Native,

00:03:56.210 --> 00:03:59.490
or it could also be called
Twelve-Factor architectures.

00:03:59.490 --> 00:04:02.330
When you build for these things
that can dynamically scale,

00:04:02.330 --> 00:04:06.980
can go from zero to as many
backing servers as you need,

00:04:06.980 --> 00:04:09.600
then your architects
needs to support that.

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:13.550
So it needs to be able to
handle that up and down,

00:04:13.550 --> 00:04:14.930
that fluctuation.

00:04:14.930 --> 00:04:18.620
And there's certain
architectural things

00:04:18.620 --> 00:04:19.980
that make this easier.

00:04:19.980 --> 00:04:21.589
So for instance,
if you don't have

00:04:21.589 --> 00:04:25.070
any state in your actual
application or service,

00:04:25.070 --> 00:04:27.800
that's one thing that allows
you to easily scale up and down.

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:29.510
You move that state
back to a database,

00:04:29.510 --> 00:04:32.280
back to an external
cache that sort of thing.

00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:35.240
And so Twelve-Factor
and Cloud Native

00:04:35.240 --> 00:04:37.190
are going to really be
the thing that enables

00:04:37.190 --> 00:04:39.320
you to embrace Serverless.

00:04:39.320 --> 00:04:41.360
And that's great, whether
you're on Kubernetes

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:44.870
or on Cloud Run or really
any serverless platform.

00:04:44.870 --> 00:04:47.570
MARTIN OMANDER: And I think
working with a large retailer,

00:04:47.570 --> 00:04:51.020
right now, and they're using
Serverless because they're

00:04:51.020 --> 00:04:53.180
large retailer but small team.

00:04:53.180 --> 00:04:54.950
They can't afford to pay--

00:04:54.950 --> 00:04:58.250
have lots and lots of ops
people monitoring servers.

00:04:58.250 --> 00:05:03.020
And they want to build stuff
and enable more services,

00:05:03.020 --> 00:05:05.150
not sit around and
monitor services.

00:05:05.150 --> 00:05:07.280
So they've gone serverless
for that reason,

00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:09.235
to make their own
team more efficient.

00:05:09.235 --> 00:05:10.610
STEPHANIE WONG:
Yeah, and I think

00:05:10.610 --> 00:05:12.740
this is a good segue
into our next question

00:05:12.740 --> 00:05:15.770
whose from,
@maaanonion, who asks,

00:05:15.770 --> 00:05:18.530
"What's the difference between
Serverless and Cloud Native?"

00:05:18.530 --> 00:05:21.377
I think we hear both
of these terms a lot

00:05:21.377 --> 00:05:23.210
and I don't know if
they're interchangeable,

00:05:23.210 --> 00:05:25.850
but it sounds like
they're related.

00:05:25.850 --> 00:05:27.260
James, what do you think?

00:05:27.260 --> 00:05:29.780
JAMES WARD: Yeah, they're
definitely related.

00:05:29.780 --> 00:05:32.510
I look at Serverless more
of, as Martin was saying,

00:05:32.510 --> 00:05:36.830
the like operational model
of Cloud Native applications.

00:05:36.830 --> 00:05:39.348
You certainly can do Cloud
Native without Serverless,

00:05:39.348 --> 00:05:41.390
without going full
serverless, and without having

00:05:41.390 --> 00:05:44.120
that dynamic scaling and
the managed platform.

00:05:44.120 --> 00:05:45.950
But if you're Cloud
Native, then it's

00:05:45.950 --> 00:05:50.460
going to make it a lot easier
for you to also be serverless.

00:05:50.460 --> 00:05:53.600
If you're starting
with non Cloud Native,

00:05:53.600 --> 00:05:55.940
first focus on
being Cloud Native.

00:05:55.940 --> 00:05:57.620
And then that will
enable you to also

00:05:57.620 --> 00:06:00.650
be serverless for the
operational aspects to that.

00:06:00.650 --> 00:06:02.960
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah, and
here's a personal question.

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:05.090
You were talking about
moving state outside

00:06:05.090 --> 00:06:06.320
of the application logic.

00:06:06.320 --> 00:06:09.080
How difficult is it, in
your opinion, for companies

00:06:09.080 --> 00:06:11.715
who may have a
monolithic app or legacy

00:06:11.715 --> 00:06:13.340
applications to move
to more of a Cloud

00:06:13.340 --> 00:06:15.290
Native or Serverless model?

00:06:15.290 --> 00:06:17.180
JAMES WARD: It can
definitely be challenging,

00:06:17.180 --> 00:06:20.420
because we traditionally
have architected applications

00:06:20.420 --> 00:06:22.910
to be these what we call--

00:06:28.460 --> 00:06:31.430
or no, we want to treat
them as cattle not pets,

00:06:31.430 --> 00:06:34.070
is the terminology
that we've heard used.

00:06:34.070 --> 00:06:36.890
And it's kind of a mean way to
talk about cattle because we

00:06:36.890 --> 00:06:41.410
love cows, but the idea
is that with your pets

00:06:41.410 --> 00:06:43.820
you really love your pet
dearly and you don't ever

00:06:43.820 --> 00:06:46.380
want that pet to go away.

00:06:46.380 --> 00:06:49.100
And so with our
traditional architectures,

00:06:49.100 --> 00:06:52.790
we would make sure that the
server should never go down.

00:06:52.790 --> 00:06:55.310
You try to keep that
thing running forever.

00:06:55.310 --> 00:06:57.410
And if it goes down
you have an outage.

00:06:57.410 --> 00:07:00.230
Whereas in Cloud Native,
we're actually architecting

00:07:00.230 --> 00:07:02.450
to support the ability to fail.

00:07:02.450 --> 00:07:06.410
Because we know that at
the scale and at the--

00:07:06.410 --> 00:07:10.430
when we move our architecture
to huge data centers

00:07:10.430 --> 00:07:12.680
that are managing
millions of machines

00:07:12.680 --> 00:07:15.620
that things may
break at that scale.

00:07:15.620 --> 00:07:17.750
And so we have to
architect for that.

00:07:17.750 --> 00:07:20.000
And so that's one of the key
things about Cloud Native

00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:21.740
is moving that state out.

00:07:21.740 --> 00:07:25.430
And so it can be a huge
challenge to going Cloud Native

00:07:25.430 --> 00:07:28.460
and going serverless, just
things like the file system.

00:07:28.460 --> 00:07:31.130
In Serverless and Cloud
Native, you typically

00:07:31.130 --> 00:07:33.410
don't write files
to the file system

00:07:33.410 --> 00:07:37.950
expecting them to be there if
that server instance goes down.

00:07:37.950 --> 00:07:40.340
So that means you then have
to use an external data

00:07:40.340 --> 00:07:42.020
store to store your files on.

00:07:42.020 --> 00:07:45.620
And of course, you can use the
Google Cloud Storage service

00:07:45.620 --> 00:07:48.115
if you're on Google
Cloud, or other options

00:07:48.115 --> 00:07:50.240
as well depending on where
you are, just for files.

00:07:50.240 --> 00:07:52.430
But then databases
are another example

00:07:52.430 --> 00:07:55.970
where you don't want to run
your-- back in the LAMPs stack

00:07:55.970 --> 00:07:58.760
days we would run our Apache
server and/or MySQL server

00:07:58.760 --> 00:08:00.560
on the same actual server.

00:08:00.560 --> 00:08:03.350
And if that thing went down or
the hard drive is corrupted,

00:08:03.350 --> 00:08:04.620
you're in big trouble.

00:08:04.620 --> 00:08:06.320
Now we need to take
those databases

00:08:06.320 --> 00:08:08.940
and also move them out to
an external data store.

00:08:08.940 --> 00:08:11.930
So you could use things
like Cloud SQL as one way

00:08:11.930 --> 00:08:13.820
to externalize
those data storage.

00:08:13.820 --> 00:08:16.880
But that allows you then to
have an elastic application

00:08:16.880 --> 00:08:20.540
or service here that can then
talk back to that backend data

00:08:20.540 --> 00:08:22.455
store.

00:08:22.455 --> 00:08:24.080
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah,
I think it speaks

00:08:24.080 --> 00:08:26.960
to this idea of modularization
and ensuring that you're

00:08:26.960 --> 00:08:28.440
separating compute and storage.

00:08:28.440 --> 00:08:30.190
So that you can scale
both of those things

00:08:30.190 --> 00:08:33.860
independently and run in a
very distributed environment.

00:08:33.860 --> 00:08:36.380
OK, so we have a lot
of Serverless products

00:08:36.380 --> 00:08:39.950
at Google Cloud or the top three
that we always speak about.

00:08:39.950 --> 00:08:44.540
But @Loppy2345, asks here,
"Please explain the difference

00:08:44.540 --> 00:08:48.020
between App Engine and
Cloud Run and which one

00:08:48.020 --> 00:08:49.170
we should be using?

00:08:49.170 --> 00:08:51.473
And this is actually one of
our more popular questions

00:08:51.473 --> 00:08:52.890
we've gotten from
multiple people.

00:08:52.890 --> 00:08:56.030
So, Martin, why don't you
go ahead and take this one?

00:08:56.030 --> 00:08:58.800
MARTIN OMANDER: Yeah, I
think actually over time,

00:08:58.800 --> 00:09:00.680
we have seen App
Engine and Cloud Run

00:09:00.680 --> 00:09:02.570
becoming more and more similar.

00:09:02.570 --> 00:09:08.450
Because as we are all learning
about Cloud Native and Cloud

00:09:08.450 --> 00:09:11.000
providers, like Google, and
developers, like you guys,

00:09:11.000 --> 00:09:14.450
we're all adopting
these best practices

00:09:14.450 --> 00:09:16.410
that James talked
about a minute ago.

00:09:16.410 --> 00:09:19.370
So because App
Engine and Cloud Run

00:09:19.370 --> 00:09:21.320
adopt all of those
best practices,

00:09:21.320 --> 00:09:22.940
they're fairly
similar I would say.

00:09:22.940 --> 00:09:26.330
And if I start a new
project I think about,

00:09:26.330 --> 00:09:27.950
what does the team know?

00:09:27.950 --> 00:09:30.140
Does the team know
either of these products?

00:09:30.140 --> 00:09:32.060
Then pick that product.

00:09:32.060 --> 00:09:34.550
If the team knows
both or neither,

00:09:34.550 --> 00:09:38.360
I guess I would use
Cloud Run if I already

00:09:38.360 --> 00:09:42.260
have a container-based developer
workflow, if I like containers.

00:09:42.260 --> 00:09:45.260
Some companies do that
because in Engine you

00:09:45.260 --> 00:09:46.220
don't have containers.

00:09:46.220 --> 00:09:49.100
In Cloud Run you can have
containers if you want to.

00:09:49.100 --> 00:09:51.643
But both are perfectly
good choices.

00:09:51.643 --> 00:09:53.060
STEPHANIE WONG:
Yeah, and is there

00:09:53.060 --> 00:09:56.703
a path from App Engine to Cloud
Run if somebody was interested?

00:09:56.703 --> 00:09:58.370
MARTIN OMANDER: Yeah,
you're typically--

00:09:58.370 --> 00:09:59.900
in App Engine for
example, you would

00:09:59.900 --> 00:10:03.740
write a Node app or
a Python native app

00:10:03.740 --> 00:10:05.090
and deploy to App Engine.

00:10:05.090 --> 00:10:08.780
And you really don't
have to change anything,

00:10:08.780 --> 00:10:13.340
or I guess a few lines at the
top of your file to move it

00:10:13.340 --> 00:10:14.820
from App Engine to Cloud Run.

00:10:14.820 --> 00:10:16.662
So it's fairly easy.

00:10:16.662 --> 00:10:18.620
JAMES WARD: Yeah, one of
the biggest challenges

00:10:18.620 --> 00:10:21.290
there is just to go into
a container-based, Docker

00:10:21.290 --> 00:10:23.810
container-based
packaging method.

00:10:23.810 --> 00:10:26.580
And there [? are ?] some
easy ways to do that.

00:10:26.580 --> 00:10:29.720
So there's something called
Buildpacks and we have

00:10:29.720 --> 00:10:31.460
the Google Cloud Buildpacks.

00:10:31.460 --> 00:10:34.622
And you can take an
existing source code base

00:10:34.622 --> 00:10:36.080
and give it to
Buildpacks and it'll

00:10:36.080 --> 00:10:37.610
create a container for you.

00:10:37.610 --> 00:10:39.380
If you need more
flexibility or you

00:10:39.380 --> 00:10:41.840
want to have more
control over the process,

00:10:41.840 --> 00:10:44.030
then you can use a
Docker file as the way

00:10:44.030 --> 00:10:46.670
to define how your
container gets created.

00:10:46.670 --> 00:10:48.830
But both of those are now
supported by Cloud Run,

00:10:48.830 --> 00:10:50.810
so when you do a
source-based deployment

00:10:50.810 --> 00:10:54.260
with Cloud Run similar to
how you do it on App Engine,

00:10:54.260 --> 00:10:57.470
we will use a Docker file if it
exists to create the container

00:10:57.470 --> 00:10:59.240
or we'll use Buildpacks
to fall back to.

00:10:59.240 --> 00:11:00.140
STEPHANIE WONG:
Awesome, So it sounds

00:11:00.140 --> 00:11:02.060
like there are tools
that people can

00:11:02.060 --> 00:11:04.100
use if they're interested
in transitioning over.

00:11:04.100 --> 00:11:07.340
One of the other questions that
we get often about Cloud Run

00:11:07.340 --> 00:11:10.970
is, "Does somebody need to
truly understand containers

00:11:10.970 --> 00:11:14.510
to use Cloud Run whatsoever?

00:11:14.510 --> 00:11:17.502
Martin and James, I'm sure you
both have opinions on this.

00:11:17.502 --> 00:11:18.710
MARTIN OMANDER: Yeah, I mean.

00:11:18.710 --> 00:11:21.630
So it used to be that you
had to understand containers

00:11:21.630 --> 00:11:24.140
and you had to
create a Docker file.

00:11:24.140 --> 00:11:29.360
But now you can do
gcloud run deploy source.

00:11:29.360 --> 00:11:32.420
And you just deploy like your
Native Node app, or Python app,

00:11:32.420 --> 00:11:36.020
or Java app and it's
deployed from source.

00:11:36.020 --> 00:11:39.260
And under the covers it's all
containers on Google [? site ?]

00:11:39.260 --> 00:11:41.790
but you may or may
not see the container.

00:11:41.790 --> 00:11:44.090
So depending on if
you like containers

00:11:44.090 --> 00:11:46.550
you ca have your own Docker
file and do that stuff.

00:11:46.550 --> 00:11:49.070
If you don't like containers
just deployed from source.

00:11:49.070 --> 00:11:50.510
But it's all
containers over here.

00:11:50.510 --> 00:11:52.427
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah, I
feel like that's worth

00:11:52.427 --> 00:11:54.890
restating just how
easy the experience can

00:11:54.890 --> 00:11:57.530
be to build from
source for somebody

00:11:57.530 --> 00:12:00.170
who may not want to understand
containers at that level.

00:12:00.170 --> 00:12:02.720
I think it just speaks
to how we're continuously

00:12:02.720 --> 00:12:04.730
creating higher
levels of abstraction

00:12:04.730 --> 00:12:06.650
for those who do want
to focus specifically

00:12:06.650 --> 00:12:08.968
on just the application logic.

00:12:08.968 --> 00:12:11.510
JAMES WARD: The nice thing is
that containers are still there

00:12:11.510 --> 00:12:12.927
and they're
underneath the covers.

00:12:12.927 --> 00:12:15.020
So you get that
portability that containers

00:12:15.020 --> 00:12:18.320
gives you, where you can
take that same container that

00:12:18.320 --> 00:12:20.150
may have been transparently
created for you

00:12:20.150 --> 00:12:22.460
and run it on Kubernetes,
run it via Docker

00:12:22.460 --> 00:12:23.720
on your local machine.

00:12:23.720 --> 00:12:25.160
Wherever Docker
container runs you

00:12:25.160 --> 00:12:27.560
can run that container, which is
nice to have that portability.

00:12:27.560 --> 00:12:29.487
So it's still containers
underneath the covers

00:12:29.487 --> 00:12:31.820
but you don't necessarily
need to think about containers

00:12:31.820 --> 00:12:32.862
and how they get created.

00:12:32.862 --> 00:12:35.070
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah, you
can reap the same benefits.

00:12:35.070 --> 00:12:35.810
That's awesome.

00:12:35.810 --> 00:12:37.430
So with the next question @JimH.

00:12:37.430 --> 00:12:40.040
This is a little
particular, but he

00:12:40.040 --> 00:12:43.970
wants to share his Python apps
APIs with his friends using

00:12:43.970 --> 00:12:45.835
Google Cloud Run,
but he is completely

00:12:45.835 --> 00:12:47.210
unfamiliar with
containers or how

00:12:47.210 --> 00:12:49.520
he might package his
Python or Windows API

00:12:49.520 --> 00:12:51.860
so he can take advantage
of Google Cloud Run.

00:12:51.860 --> 00:12:52.790
Where does he start?

00:12:52.790 --> 00:12:55.040
Similar to, I think, what
we just talked about, James.

00:12:55.040 --> 00:12:56.960
You said Buildpacks
is one option.

00:12:56.960 --> 00:12:58.913
Anything else here to add?

00:12:58.913 --> 00:13:00.830
JAMES WARD: Yeah I think
Buildpacks are really

00:13:00.830 --> 00:13:03.740
the transparent way to go
from that Python source code

00:13:03.740 --> 00:13:05.360
to a container.

00:13:05.360 --> 00:13:08.780
Buildpacks are a Cloud Native
foundation-- under the Cloud

00:13:08.780 --> 00:13:10.490
Native Computing
Foundation, they're

00:13:10.490 --> 00:13:12.170
called Cloud Native Buildpacks.

00:13:12.170 --> 00:13:15.140
So this is an open
standard and Google Cloud

00:13:15.140 --> 00:13:16.310
has built our Buildpacks.

00:13:16.310 --> 00:13:18.470
We have samples on our GitHub.

00:13:18.470 --> 00:13:20.720
I think it's just called
Buildpacks samples

00:13:20.720 --> 00:13:22.620
and of course there's
a Python sample there.

00:13:22.620 --> 00:13:24.140
So check that one out.

00:13:24.140 --> 00:13:25.900
And you'll see that
with Python there

00:13:25.900 --> 00:13:27.900
is one little trick that
you need to know about,

00:13:27.900 --> 00:13:31.190
which is you need to tell the
Buildpacks how to actually

00:13:31.190 --> 00:13:32.297
start your application.

00:13:32.297 --> 00:13:34.130
And so there's something
called a proc file,

00:13:34.130 --> 00:13:35.550
is one way to do that.

00:13:35.550 --> 00:13:39.020
And so that's one thing
that Python doesn't give us

00:13:39.020 --> 00:13:42.500
for Buildpacks is the ability to
know what application to start

00:13:42.500 --> 00:13:43.610
in your Python code.

00:13:43.610 --> 00:13:45.230
Whereas in Node,
this is something

00:13:45.230 --> 00:13:48.410
that you do through an NPM start
command and other frameworks

00:13:48.410 --> 00:13:50.090
have different ways
for the Buildpacks

00:13:50.090 --> 00:13:51.200
to know what command to start.

00:13:51.200 --> 00:13:53.580
But with Python we don't know
how to start your application.

00:13:53.580 --> 00:13:55.330
So that's the one
little thing that you're

00:13:55.330 --> 00:13:57.200
going to have to add
in potentially from--

00:13:57.200 --> 00:13:58.820
versus what you have now.

00:13:58.820 --> 00:14:01.070
But yeah, check out the
Buildpacks samples for Python.

00:14:01.070 --> 00:14:03.785
That should be pretty
helpful to get you going.

00:14:03.785 --> 00:14:05.160
STEPHANIE WONG:
Perfect, awesome.

00:14:05.160 --> 00:14:07.710
OK so next question
is from @lceus.

00:14:07.710 --> 00:14:11.880
"Could you explain how to
set up Cloud Run on Anthos?"

00:14:11.880 --> 00:14:13.450
James, do you want to start?

00:14:13.450 --> 00:14:14.280
JAMES WARD: Sure.

00:14:14.280 --> 00:14:17.040
Yeah so what's called
Cloud Run for Anthos

00:14:17.040 --> 00:14:20.640
is a way to have the Cloud Run
experience on Anthos, which

00:14:20.640 --> 00:14:26.790
is a way to be multi-cloud, be
on-prem with GKE or Kubernetes

00:14:26.790 --> 00:14:27.390
Engine.

00:14:27.390 --> 00:14:29.037
And so with Cloud
Run for Anthos,

00:14:29.037 --> 00:14:30.870
you get all the great
things about Cloud Run

00:14:30.870 --> 00:14:33.240
but you get them on top of GKE.

00:14:33.240 --> 00:14:37.400
And so you can use this
either in our GKE service.

00:14:37.400 --> 00:14:39.150
You could use it on
other Cloud providers,

00:14:39.150 --> 00:14:40.530
you could use it on-prem.

00:14:40.530 --> 00:14:43.980
And to get started you
first need to have Anthos.

00:14:43.980 --> 00:14:46.110
And so if you're in the
Google Cloud console,

00:14:46.110 --> 00:14:47.700
if you actually
go into Cloud Run

00:14:47.700 --> 00:14:50.550
and you go try to deploy an
application, which is-- just

00:14:50.550 --> 00:14:53.760
say-- like create service,
then you click on Anthos

00:14:53.760 --> 00:14:54.990
as the deployment target.

00:14:54.990 --> 00:14:57.750
If you don't already have
an Anthos cluster set up,

00:14:57.750 --> 00:14:59.610
then it will actually
help you walk through

00:14:59.610 --> 00:15:01.650
and create that Anthos cluster.

00:15:01.650 --> 00:15:03.900
There are other ways to
create an Anthos cluster

00:15:03.900 --> 00:15:04.990
on Google Cloud as well.

00:15:04.990 --> 00:15:06.640
And once you get
an Anthos cluster,

00:15:06.640 --> 00:15:09.938
you can then deploy to it
through the Cloud Run UI

00:15:09.938 --> 00:15:11.730
or through the typical
ways that you deploy

00:15:11.730 --> 00:15:12.990
to Cloud Run, which is like--

00:15:12.990 --> 00:15:14.130
command line is one option.

00:15:14.130 --> 00:15:17.160
You do gcloud run deploy
to deploy your application.

00:15:17.160 --> 00:15:20.700
You can do that to deploy to
Cloud Run for Anthos as well.

00:15:20.700 --> 00:15:26.820
But start with the console,
console.cloud.google.com.

00:15:26.820 --> 00:15:30.630
and just go to Cloud Run and
pick Anthos as the target

00:15:30.630 --> 00:15:33.570
and should be pretty
straightforward to deploy

00:15:33.570 --> 00:15:34.615
from there.

00:15:34.615 --> 00:15:35.490
STEPHANIE WONG: Cool.

00:15:35.490 --> 00:15:38.160
Sounds like there's a couple
options there to get started

00:15:38.160 --> 00:15:40.230
with Cloud Run for Anthos.

00:15:40.230 --> 00:15:43.530
Our next question is
from @DheerajP, who asks,

00:15:43.530 --> 00:15:46.290
"Do the environment variables
in the Cloud Run dashboard

00:15:46.290 --> 00:15:50.730
allow KMS encrypted values to
be specified instead of publicly

00:15:50.730 --> 00:15:53.160
exposing database credentials?"

00:15:53.160 --> 00:15:55.200
Martin, what about you?

00:15:55.200 --> 00:15:56.760
MARTIN OMANDER: Yeah, so yes.

00:15:56.760 --> 00:15:58.200
So first off, great question.

00:15:58.200 --> 00:16:00.690
And the background here is--

00:16:00.690 --> 00:16:03.630
I see that the word
public is used here.

00:16:03.630 --> 00:16:05.970
If you have
environment-- well, so it

00:16:05.970 --> 00:16:08.515
used to be for
years and years it

00:16:08.515 --> 00:16:11.670
has just being considered
best practice to have things

00:16:11.670 --> 00:16:15.570
like database passwords,
file paths, and things

00:16:15.570 --> 00:16:17.170
like that as
environment variables.

00:16:17.170 --> 00:16:19.200
So they are not
hard-coded in your code.

00:16:19.200 --> 00:16:21.300
That is a great practice.

00:16:21.300 --> 00:16:25.510
Now if you [INAUDIBLE]---- so
people have started saying,

00:16:25.510 --> 00:16:29.910
well, actually if I have it
in environment variables then

00:16:29.910 --> 00:16:32.640
if part of my application--
maybe I have a malicious

00:16:32.640 --> 00:16:36.030
library somewhere in my
application that I downloaded

00:16:36.030 --> 00:16:38.190
open source something.

00:16:38.190 --> 00:16:40.210
It could read
environmental variables.

00:16:40.210 --> 00:16:44.230
So I want to be even
more secure than that.

00:16:44.230 --> 00:16:47.280
And that is, I think, is where
this question comes from.

00:16:47.280 --> 00:16:49.500
So if you use environment
variables in Cloud Run,

00:16:49.500 --> 00:16:50.490
they're not public.

00:16:50.490 --> 00:16:52.710
It's only you can see them.

00:16:52.710 --> 00:16:54.390
But you could
potentially have a piece

00:16:54.390 --> 00:16:56.670
of malicious code
inside your application

00:16:56.670 --> 00:16:58.440
that can read
environmental variables.

00:16:58.440 --> 00:17:00.270
And the way to get
around that-- of course

00:17:00.270 --> 00:17:02.260
this is, of course,
a common problem.

00:17:02.260 --> 00:17:06.300
So to get around that you use
Secret Manager, which is part

00:17:06.300 --> 00:17:07.890
of your Google Cloud account.

00:17:07.890 --> 00:17:11.310
You just turn it on and
that is a very secure way

00:17:11.310 --> 00:17:13.859
of storing these secrets
like database passwords

00:17:13.859 --> 00:17:15.869
and so on, if you
want to do something

00:17:15.869 --> 00:17:19.740
that's even more secure
than having just environment

00:17:19.740 --> 00:17:20.310
variables.

00:17:20.310 --> 00:17:22.410
And you can set
things up so they're

00:17:22.410 --> 00:17:24.842
audited and all that good
stuff in Secret Manager.

00:17:24.842 --> 00:17:26.550
STEPHANIE WONG: Right,
and just for those

00:17:26.550 --> 00:17:28.050
who haven't heard
of Secret Manager,

00:17:28.050 --> 00:17:30.180
it's a secure and
convenient storage system

00:17:30.180 --> 00:17:32.820
for API keys,
passwords, certificates,

00:17:32.820 --> 00:17:33.780
other sensitive data.

00:17:33.780 --> 00:17:36.930
And it gives you this central
place and source of truth

00:17:36.930 --> 00:17:39.030
to manage all of these
things and audit secrets

00:17:39.030 --> 00:17:40.560
across Google Cloud.

00:17:40.560 --> 00:17:42.270
So definitely a
great tool to use.

00:17:42.270 --> 00:17:46.150
OK, so next question comes
from @Jorgel who asks,

00:17:46.150 --> 00:17:48.930
"Do you have any tips on how
to accurately estimate costs

00:17:48.930 --> 00:17:52.740
for using Cloud Run
in this way? " James,

00:17:52.740 --> 00:17:54.840
I'm going to you for this one.

00:17:54.840 --> 00:17:57.960
JAMES WARD: Yeah, so cost
estimation was something

00:17:57.960 --> 00:18:00.210
that's elastic, can be hard.

00:18:00.210 --> 00:18:03.450
This is like your power
bill, your energy bill

00:18:03.450 --> 00:18:06.060
that you get from
the power company.

00:18:06.060 --> 00:18:08.070
How much is the bill
going to be next month?

00:18:08.070 --> 00:18:08.730
I don't know.

00:18:08.730 --> 00:18:10.200
It depends on how much you use.

00:18:10.200 --> 00:18:13.320
And so usually the way
that we do estimation,

00:18:13.320 --> 00:18:15.870
just like you probably do
estimation of what your energy

00:18:15.870 --> 00:18:17.730
bill is going to
be, is you base that

00:18:17.730 --> 00:18:19.630
on what you did in the past.

00:18:19.630 --> 00:18:23.190
And so based on the
past bill, that's

00:18:23.190 --> 00:18:24.810
how you can determine likely--

00:18:24.810 --> 00:18:27.540
or the ballpark of
your future bill.

00:18:27.540 --> 00:18:30.060
So usually the way that
we do cost estimation

00:18:30.060 --> 00:18:33.750
when you're in a environment
that is scalable like Cloud

00:18:33.750 --> 00:18:38.100
Run, is you do a test
that tries to simulate

00:18:38.100 --> 00:18:40.320
what your actual
traffic is going to be.

00:18:40.320 --> 00:18:43.140
And then you take
that test and you then

00:18:43.140 --> 00:18:45.690
see, all right, how much money
did we spend with that test.

00:18:45.690 --> 00:18:47.400
And then you can
extrapolate from there

00:18:47.400 --> 00:18:50.610
how much money it's going to
be when you're actually live.

00:18:50.610 --> 00:18:54.060
There is a cost calculator that
you can use on the Google Cloud

00:18:54.060 --> 00:18:55.080
website too.

00:18:55.080 --> 00:18:59.358
But this is not exactly going
to give you a real world

00:18:59.358 --> 00:19:00.900
picture, because
you're going to have

00:19:00.900 --> 00:19:03.720
to estimate how many
concurrent connections are

00:19:03.720 --> 00:19:04.770
going to be coming in.

00:19:04.770 --> 00:19:06.990
Which could be really
hard to actually estimate

00:19:06.990 --> 00:19:10.370
in your real world usage
of your backend service

00:19:10.370 --> 00:19:12.230
or your application.

00:19:12.230 --> 00:19:16.020
So really, if you want to
get the most accurate number,

00:19:16.020 --> 00:19:17.603
you need to actually
run a simulation.

00:19:17.603 --> 00:19:20.228
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah, it sounds
like a chicken and egg problem.

00:19:20.228 --> 00:19:22.960
You want to find out, but you
also need to test it out first.

00:19:22.960 --> 00:19:26.960
But do you find that there's a
certain size in this simulation

00:19:26.960 --> 00:19:28.710
that you need to run
through to experiment

00:19:28.710 --> 00:19:29.835
and be able to extrapolate?

00:19:29.835 --> 00:19:33.060
For example, scale
up to thousands

00:19:33.060 --> 00:19:36.540
of concurrent connections or
is there a certain minimum

00:19:36.540 --> 00:19:38.580
that you think that
people need to run through

00:19:38.580 --> 00:19:39.923
for the simulation?

00:19:39.923 --> 00:19:41.340
JAMES WARD: One
of the hard things

00:19:41.340 --> 00:19:43.132
is that there's a lot
of knobs that you can

00:19:43.132 --> 00:19:45.670
turn to change the behavior.

00:19:45.670 --> 00:19:48.420
So with Cloud Run
the default number

00:19:48.420 --> 00:19:51.510
of concurrent connections
to a given instance is 80.

00:19:51.510 --> 00:19:53.730
You can turn that
down to as few as one

00:19:53.730 --> 00:19:57.070
or up to as many as 250
concurrent connections.

00:19:57.070 --> 00:19:59.670
And this can dramatically
impact your bill,

00:19:59.670 --> 00:20:02.350
but it can also dramatically
impact your performance.

00:20:02.350 --> 00:20:05.340
So if you turn it all the way
up to 250, then that's great.

00:20:05.340 --> 00:20:08.010
That means you're going to
get 250 concurrent connections

00:20:08.010 --> 00:20:09.750
to a single instance
and you only

00:20:09.750 --> 00:20:13.890
get billed the CPU and
memory time for one instance

00:20:13.890 --> 00:20:16.820
across those 250
concurrent connections.

00:20:16.820 --> 00:20:21.270
But if your workloads are
very IO and CPU intensive,

00:20:21.270 --> 00:20:25.230
then you may actually be causing
a huge performance degradation

00:20:25.230 --> 00:20:26.410
by doing this.

00:20:26.410 --> 00:20:30.840
Which then may send more
request to other instances,

00:20:30.840 --> 00:20:33.690
because they can't handle those
requests potentially as fast.

00:20:33.690 --> 00:20:37.110
And so it's really with
simulation testing--

00:20:37.110 --> 00:20:39.570
you want to try to
actually make the load look

00:20:39.570 --> 00:20:42.970
as similar to the real world
load that you're going to have.

00:20:42.970 --> 00:20:44.460
And then turn some
of those knobs

00:20:44.460 --> 00:20:47.160
and see, if I turn the number
of concurrent connections up

00:20:47.160 --> 00:20:49.937
or I turn the number of CPUs
up or whatever it may be,

00:20:49.937 --> 00:20:51.520
then it's going to
change some things.

00:20:51.520 --> 00:20:53.437
And so you're going to
have to do some testing

00:20:53.437 --> 00:20:55.502
to figure out what the
optimal configuration is.

00:20:55.502 --> 00:20:57.960
It's actually one of the really
nice things about Cloud Run

00:20:57.960 --> 00:20:59.797
that you can have
concurrent connections.

00:20:59.797 --> 00:21:01.380
Some of the other
serverless platforms

00:21:01.380 --> 00:21:03.780
actually don't allow you to
have concurrent connections,

00:21:03.780 --> 00:21:06.280
multiple concurrent connections,
and so then you're actually

00:21:06.280 --> 00:21:07.650
paying like per request.

00:21:07.650 --> 00:21:10.080
You're not getting
the advantage of being

00:21:10.080 --> 00:21:13.410
able to handle many connections
on a single instance

00:21:13.410 --> 00:21:15.490
and only get billed
for that one instance.

00:21:15.490 --> 00:21:16.110
MARTIN OMANDER: And also--

00:21:16.110 --> 00:21:17.250
STEPHANIE WONG: Right, right.

00:21:17.250 --> 00:21:19.083
MARTIN OMANDER: --and
also in my experience,

00:21:19.083 --> 00:21:21.900
the real world applications
I've worked on, it's

00:21:21.900 --> 00:21:26.060
seldom Cloud Run that
is the big dollar bill.

00:21:26.060 --> 00:21:29.440
It is usually
bandwidth or databases.

00:21:29.440 --> 00:21:32.280
And that stuff you're going to
have whether you do serverless

00:21:32.280 --> 00:21:33.730
or not.

00:21:33.730 --> 00:21:37.320
And so, yeah, in my experience
most of the money lands there.

00:21:37.320 --> 00:21:40.290
You should perhaps
look at-- make

00:21:40.290 --> 00:21:42.480
sure you include bandwidth
and databases in when

00:21:42.480 --> 00:21:43.908
you make estimates.

00:21:43.908 --> 00:21:45.450
STEPHANIE WONG:
Yeah, important to be

00:21:45.450 --> 00:21:48.880
able to isolate the issue
during this testing phase.

00:21:48.880 --> 00:21:52.020
So our next question
is from @VildanC

00:21:52.020 --> 00:21:55.020
who asks, "What if I want to
access my private Google Cloud

00:21:55.020 --> 00:21:57.570
storage from Cloud Run?

00:21:57.570 --> 00:21:59.050
Martin, won't you take this one?

00:21:59.050 --> 00:22:02.610
MARTIN OMANDER: Yeah, so I'm
not sure what Vildan means here

00:22:02.610 --> 00:22:06.720
but it could be either I want
to access a Google Cloud storage

00:22:06.720 --> 00:22:08.970
bucket in GCP that's
private to me.

00:22:08.970 --> 00:22:12.780
Or it could be that maybe
Vildan means the Google Drive

00:22:12.780 --> 00:22:13.960
private storage.

00:22:13.960 --> 00:22:16.650
So if it's a Google
Cloud storage

00:22:16.650 --> 00:22:19.560
bucket that is private to--

00:22:19.560 --> 00:22:21.350
only Vildan can see it.

00:22:21.350 --> 00:22:24.240
What Vildan needs
to do is to find

00:22:24.240 --> 00:22:33.900
the name of that Compute Engine
account that Cloud Run, runs as

00:22:33.900 --> 00:22:39.810
and then you can find
that in the Cloud console.

00:22:39.810 --> 00:22:42.030
That looks like an email
address and then give

00:22:42.030 --> 00:22:46.350
access for that account to
this Cloud Storage bucket.

00:22:46.350 --> 00:22:49.470
And then you would read,
using the Cloud storage API,

00:22:49.470 --> 00:22:52.007
it's a library you can download.

00:22:52.007 --> 00:22:52.590
And actually--

00:22:52.590 --> 00:22:54.473
STEPHANIE WONG: Right,
and you're referring.

00:22:54.473 --> 00:22:55.140
Sorry, go ahead.

00:22:55.140 --> 00:22:56.850
MARTIN OMANDER: Yeah and
actually it's the same thing.

00:22:56.850 --> 00:22:58.770
If it's Google Drive
we're talking about,

00:22:58.770 --> 00:23:00.240
I've written many
applications that

00:23:00.240 --> 00:23:02.240
access my private to drive.

00:23:02.240 --> 00:23:06.180
Then again, I find that
Compute Engine account.

00:23:06.180 --> 00:23:07.650
It looks like an email address.

00:23:07.650 --> 00:23:10.710
I share a folder
in my Google Drive

00:23:10.710 --> 00:23:12.420
or an individual spreadsheet.

00:23:12.420 --> 00:23:17.400
I click share, paste that in
there, and give it read access.

00:23:17.400 --> 00:23:19.420
And now my Cloud
Run can read that.

00:23:19.420 --> 00:23:22.290
And just like with
Cloud Storage,

00:23:22.290 --> 00:23:25.072
there is a client library
you download to access Drive.

00:23:25.072 --> 00:23:26.530
STEPHANIE WONG:
Oh, that's awesome.

00:23:26.530 --> 00:23:28.572
I didn't know that you
could use the same service

00:23:28.572 --> 00:23:32.662
account for these external
storage locations too.

00:23:32.662 --> 00:23:34.620
MARTIN OMANDER: Yeah, we
just released a video.

00:23:34.620 --> 00:23:36.300
I think, is it this
week or next week,

00:23:36.300 --> 00:23:39.180
where we actually have a
REST API running on Cloud Run

00:23:39.180 --> 00:23:41.020
and the backend is a
Google spreadsheet.

00:23:41.020 --> 00:23:43.020
So you don't have to muck
around with databases.

00:23:43.020 --> 00:23:45.990
It's far easier to have a
spreadsheet as a database

00:23:45.990 --> 00:23:48.060
and Cloud Run reads
from the spreadsheet.

00:23:48.060 --> 00:23:48.930
STEPHANIE WONG:
Yeah, that's awesome.

00:23:48.930 --> 00:23:51.305
JAMES WARD: The first time
that I added a service account

00:23:51.305 --> 00:23:56.520
to a Google Drive Doc and all
of a sudden it magically shared,

00:23:56.520 --> 00:23:57.550
I was just blown away.

00:23:57.550 --> 00:23:59.760
I was like, whoa, all these
identities are connected.

00:23:59.760 --> 00:24:00.867
And it was pretty awesome.

00:24:00.867 --> 00:24:01.950
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah, see.

00:24:01.950 --> 00:24:04.440
Even we still get blown away
even if you work with it a lot

00:24:04.440 --> 00:24:05.520
every day.

00:24:05.520 --> 00:24:08.190
And for those of you that
are listening or watching,

00:24:08.190 --> 00:24:10.440
a service account
is this special kind

00:24:10.440 --> 00:24:12.060
of account used
by an application

00:24:12.060 --> 00:24:15.640
or a virtual machine, as Martin
was explaining, not a person.

00:24:15.640 --> 00:24:17.370
So applications use
service accounts

00:24:17.370 --> 00:24:19.420
to make authorized API calls.

00:24:19.420 --> 00:24:21.420
Authorized as either the
service account itself,

00:24:21.420 --> 00:24:24.750
or as Google Workspace,
or Cloud Identity users

00:24:24.750 --> 00:24:26.940
through this
domain-wide delegation.

00:24:26.940 --> 00:24:27.450
All right.

00:24:27.450 --> 00:24:32.100
So we have another question
here by @DinakaranS

00:24:32.100 --> 00:24:35.700
who says, "I would like to see
how role based authorization

00:24:35.700 --> 00:24:37.470
can be done."

00:24:37.470 --> 00:24:40.102
James, why don't we go to you?

00:24:40.102 --> 00:24:41.060
JAMES WARD: Yeah, sure.

00:24:41.060 --> 00:24:43.470
So this is back to
the service account.

00:24:43.470 --> 00:24:45.652
So when you have
a service account

00:24:45.652 --> 00:24:48.110
there's actually two different
sides of the service account

00:24:48.110 --> 00:24:49.160
in Cloud Run.

00:24:49.160 --> 00:24:53.490
There's the service account
that the service runs as,

00:24:53.490 --> 00:24:56.150
and then you can also
assign service accounts

00:24:56.150 --> 00:24:58.230
that can talk to a service.

00:24:58.230 --> 00:25:02.300
So that's one way that you can
control who actually has access

00:25:02.300 --> 00:25:04.500
to call your Cloud Run service.

00:25:04.500 --> 00:25:07.340
So if you want to make it so
that only particular service

00:25:07.340 --> 00:25:10.250
accounts can make a call
to a Cloud Run service,

00:25:10.250 --> 00:25:11.190
you can do that.

00:25:11.190 --> 00:25:13.910
You can also give certain
people a role called,

00:25:13.910 --> 00:25:16.280
I think it's called
Cloud Run invoker role.

00:25:16.280 --> 00:25:17.910
Which you can say,
all right, Martin.

00:25:17.910 --> 00:25:20.540
You have the Cloud Run
invoker role for this service

00:25:20.540 --> 00:25:22.650
and that allows you to
then call that service.

00:25:22.650 --> 00:25:26.450
So there's a few different ways
within IAM and Google Cloud

00:25:26.450 --> 00:25:27.470
to configure it.

00:25:27.470 --> 00:25:29.810
But certainly Cloud Run
services can be locked down

00:25:29.810 --> 00:25:33.620
to specific service accounts,
specific users, specific roles

00:25:33.620 --> 00:25:36.120
that can make actual HTTP calls.

00:25:36.120 --> 00:25:38.445
And if they don't
have that, then it's

00:25:38.445 --> 00:25:40.070
actually Google Cloud
that blocks that.

00:25:40.070 --> 00:25:42.530
Your application doesn't
need to have that logic

00:25:42.530 --> 00:25:46.340
to do that block, do
that role based off--

00:25:46.340 --> 00:25:48.720
Google Cloud just
does that for you.

00:25:48.720 --> 00:25:50.270
MARTIN OMANDER: And
also and service

00:25:50.270 --> 00:25:51.812
accounts that's
great for if you have

00:25:51.812 --> 00:25:58.250
like machines, calling machines,
and machines calling services.

00:25:58.250 --> 00:26:01.040
If Dinakaran is
talking about end users

00:26:01.040 --> 00:26:04.460
coming in through a web
browser and hitting services,

00:26:04.460 --> 00:26:08.042
then you probably want
to look at Firebase off.

00:26:08.042 --> 00:26:09.500
That is one way of
dealing with it.

00:26:09.500 --> 00:26:12.020
You put Firebase,
open your servers,

00:26:12.020 --> 00:26:14.510
and now people can sign in
with their Google accounts,

00:26:14.510 --> 00:26:16.320
or Facebook account,
Apple account.

00:26:16.320 --> 00:26:17.570
There's a bunch of checkboxes.

00:26:17.570 --> 00:26:21.020
You can turn them on and off.

00:26:21.020 --> 00:26:22.850
And you should also--

00:26:22.850 --> 00:26:24.950
I want to put a bug
in your ear Dinakaran

00:26:24.950 --> 00:26:28.820
about identity aware proxy.

00:26:28.820 --> 00:26:32.870
That is also a product that can
sit in front of your App Engine

00:26:32.870 --> 00:26:33.470
apps.

00:26:33.470 --> 00:26:36.380
And you can for example
say, everybody at my company

00:26:36.380 --> 00:26:40.760
can do this, and only the
people in the finance department

00:26:40.760 --> 00:26:43.970
can do this, access
these few services.

00:26:43.970 --> 00:26:46.430
So there are many things
to choose from, but have

00:26:46.430 --> 00:26:49.632
a look at Firebase off
identity aware proxy.

00:26:49.632 --> 00:26:51.590
STEPHANIE WONG: Well,
I'm very glad you brought

00:26:51.590 --> 00:26:53.300
that up because this
is a perfect segue

00:26:53.300 --> 00:26:58.100
into our next question
who is from @NeilK

00:26:58.100 --> 00:27:02.750
and he asks, "Can we expect IAP
to support Cloud Run anytime

00:27:02.750 --> 00:27:03.250
soon?"

00:27:03.250 --> 00:27:05.042
MARTIN OMANDER: We get
this question a lot.

00:27:05.042 --> 00:27:05.960
So yes.

00:27:05.960 --> 00:27:06.520
Definitely.

00:27:06.520 --> 00:27:10.820
This is-- we're
working hard on it.

00:27:10.820 --> 00:27:12.380
STEPHANIE WONG:
Always working hard.

00:27:12.380 --> 00:27:15.020
Yes and as Martin
explained, IAP lets

00:27:15.020 --> 00:27:17.180
you use identity and
context to guard access

00:27:17.180 --> 00:27:19.160
to your applications and VMs.

00:27:19.160 --> 00:27:21.530
So you get this central
authorization layer

00:27:21.530 --> 00:27:25.262
for any applications that are
accessed by HTTPS specifically.

00:27:25.262 --> 00:27:26.720
Then you can use
application level,

00:27:26.720 --> 00:27:30.970
access control models instead
of relying on network firewalls.

00:27:30.970 --> 00:27:31.470
Awesome.

00:27:31.470 --> 00:27:32.970
MARTIN OMANDER: And
also by the way,

00:27:32.970 --> 00:27:37.340
if Neil is writing
an API specifically

00:27:37.340 --> 00:27:40.670
and it doesn't want to use IAP--

00:27:40.670 --> 00:27:43.460
well, there is also
the API Gateway

00:27:43.460 --> 00:27:45.740
that was just made
available that

00:27:45.740 --> 00:27:49.610
can do-- sit in front of your
Cloud Run services or functions

00:27:49.610 --> 00:27:52.400
or whatever and do
the authentication.

00:27:52.400 --> 00:27:57.080
So have a look and see if IAP
or API Gateway is best fit

00:27:57.080 --> 00:27:58.130
for your app.

00:27:58.130 --> 00:27:59.130
STEPHANIE WONG: Awesome.

00:27:59.130 --> 00:28:01.100
Yeah and we're going
to include hopefully

00:28:01.100 --> 00:28:04.750
the links to some of these in
the description after this.

00:28:04.750 --> 00:28:07.250
So if you want to check out IAP
and some of the other things

00:28:07.250 --> 00:28:08.750
we talk about with
service accounts,

00:28:08.750 --> 00:28:11.170
please go ahead and take a look.

00:28:11.170 --> 00:28:11.670
OK.

00:28:11.670 --> 00:28:14.690
So our next question is
from @SenjuV who asks,

00:28:14.690 --> 00:28:17.960
"Does Cloud Run
support WebSocket?"

00:28:17.960 --> 00:28:19.280
James, what about you?

00:28:19.280 --> 00:28:20.090
JAMES WARD: Sure.

00:28:20.090 --> 00:28:22.190
Yeah, so this was
actually just announced

00:28:22.190 --> 00:28:25.400
a couple of weeks ago, but Cloud
Run now generally available

00:28:25.400 --> 00:28:27.388
supports WebSockets.

00:28:27.388 --> 00:28:29.930
I think it's actually still in
beta, so maybe not [INAUDIBLE]

00:28:29.930 --> 00:28:30.430
yet.

00:28:30.430 --> 00:28:32.630
Like are two different
designations,

00:28:32.630 --> 00:28:33.440
but you can do it.

00:28:33.440 --> 00:28:35.550
You deploy a WebSocket
application today

00:28:35.550 --> 00:28:37.310
and it should just work.

00:28:37.310 --> 00:28:40.800
So there's a few different
ways that you can do this.

00:28:40.800 --> 00:28:45.170
One is that you can do the
HTTP/1.1 upgrade to WebSocket

00:28:45.170 --> 00:28:46.260
and that works fine.

00:28:46.260 --> 00:28:49.910
You can also do something
over HTTP/2 and use

00:28:49.910 --> 00:28:53.540
what's called h2c, which
is clear text HTTP/2.

00:28:53.540 --> 00:28:56.000
And Cloud Run supports
that as the mechanism

00:28:56.000 --> 00:28:58.460
to connect from essentially
the load balancer proxy

00:28:58.460 --> 00:28:59.985
and your application as well.

00:28:59.985 --> 00:29:01.610
So a number of
different options there.

00:29:01.610 --> 00:29:03.050
You could also do--

00:29:03.050 --> 00:29:05.600
server-sent events is
a popular option, which

00:29:05.600 --> 00:29:07.875
does chunked encoded responses.

00:29:07.875 --> 00:29:09.000
So that's one way to do it.

00:29:09.000 --> 00:29:11.490
And then of course
with the support,

00:29:11.490 --> 00:29:13.730
it also brings gRPC
streaming support,

00:29:13.730 --> 00:29:16.470
bidirectional gRPC
streaming support.

00:29:16.470 --> 00:29:18.200
So your Cloud Run
services can actually

00:29:18.200 --> 00:29:20.930
do bidirectional
communication on Cloud Run.

00:29:20.930 --> 00:29:22.820
And I think that
actually ends up

00:29:22.820 --> 00:29:25.460
using HTTP/2 underneath
the covers usually.

00:29:25.460 --> 00:29:28.160
But really great
support for a real time

00:29:28.160 --> 00:29:31.497
push-based applications
and services.

00:29:31.497 --> 00:29:32.580
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah, wow.

00:29:32.580 --> 00:29:34.370
And because of this
bidirectional ongoing

00:29:34.370 --> 00:29:36.020
conversation between
client and server

00:29:36.020 --> 00:29:37.437
and some of the
use cases you just

00:29:37.437 --> 00:29:40.130
mentioned, do you foresee that
this support for WebSocket

00:29:40.130 --> 00:29:42.590
does actually improve
application performance

00:29:42.590 --> 00:29:45.977
for event-driven architectures?

00:29:45.977 --> 00:29:47.810
JAMES WARD: Yeah, it
definitely is important

00:29:47.810 --> 00:29:51.710
if you're doing anything
event-driven or streaming.

00:29:51.710 --> 00:29:55.400
Where this is important
and why Cloud Run is

00:29:55.400 --> 00:29:57.620
a great fit for this,
is that typically when

00:29:57.620 --> 00:30:00.110
you have this event
streaming services

00:30:00.110 --> 00:30:02.250
you have a lot of
idle connections.

00:30:02.250 --> 00:30:03.950
And so these
connections are just

00:30:03.950 --> 00:30:05.660
sitting there waiting
for some event

00:30:05.660 --> 00:30:07.470
to send down to the client.

00:30:07.470 --> 00:30:10.070
And with Cloud Run,
because you can support up

00:30:10.070 --> 00:30:13.470
to that 250 concurrent
connections per instance,

00:30:13.470 --> 00:30:16.550
if a lot of those connections
are just idle, that's OK.

00:30:16.550 --> 00:30:18.230
You're not going
to have to spin up,

00:30:18.230 --> 00:30:20.360
let's say 250
instances, to support

00:30:20.360 --> 00:30:22.130
250 concurrent connections.

00:30:22.130 --> 00:30:24.860
You really could handle that
on a single instance and so

00:30:24.860 --> 00:30:26.760
that's going to save you money.

00:30:26.760 --> 00:30:29.330
So it's just a more efficient
way to utilize those resources.

00:30:29.330 --> 00:30:31.880
So yeah, with event-driven
architectures,

00:30:31.880 --> 00:30:34.040
the support for
streaming, and push,

00:30:34.040 --> 00:30:36.355
and bidirectional communication
on Cloud Run is great.

00:30:36.355 --> 00:30:37.230
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah.

00:30:37.230 --> 00:30:37.960
Yeah, absolutely.

00:30:37.960 --> 00:30:39.710
I think this is really
valuable for anyone

00:30:39.710 --> 00:30:41.270
who doesn't know that
concurrent connections

00:30:41.270 --> 00:30:42.830
on a single instance
are possible

00:30:42.830 --> 00:30:46.600
and that you can use it to get
the most value out of this.

00:30:46.600 --> 00:30:47.100
OK.

00:30:47.100 --> 00:30:49.940
So our next question
is from @DuL

00:30:49.940 --> 00:30:53.870
who asks, "hey, a question
about Cloud Run on GKE

00:30:53.870 --> 00:30:58.100
or Cloud Run for
Anthos, versus Knative.

00:30:58.100 --> 00:31:00.770
Is gcloud run deploy
a thin wrapper

00:31:00.770 --> 00:31:02.930
on top of kubectl apply?

00:31:02.930 --> 00:31:05.330
Can I use kubectl only?

00:31:05.330 --> 00:31:08.660
Are there any features that
gcloud run deploy provides that

00:31:08.660 --> 00:31:11.510
kubectl apply doesn't?"

00:31:11.510 --> 00:31:12.470
I will go to James.

00:31:12.470 --> 00:31:14.220
JAMES WARD: Yeah,
that's a great question.

00:31:14.220 --> 00:31:16.640
So for those that
aren't aware, Knative

00:31:16.640 --> 00:31:20.180
is a way that you can
make Kubernetes easier

00:31:20.180 --> 00:31:22.187
and make it serverless.

00:31:22.187 --> 00:31:24.020
And this is actually
what's underneath Cloud

00:31:24.020 --> 00:31:24.840
Run for Anthos.

00:31:24.840 --> 00:31:27.680
So Knative as an open source
project with a Cloud Native

00:31:27.680 --> 00:31:29.000
Computing Foundation.

00:31:29.000 --> 00:31:31.460
And because it's
based on Kubernetes,

00:31:31.460 --> 00:31:35.180
the tool to interact with
it typically is kubectl.

00:31:35.180 --> 00:31:37.820
It's the kube control, lots
of different names for it,

00:31:37.820 --> 00:31:39.920
but it's the way to
take a ymal document

00:31:39.920 --> 00:31:41.750
and give it to Kubernetes.

00:31:41.750 --> 00:31:43.760
And so when you work
with Knative or Cloud Run

00:31:43.760 --> 00:31:47.270
for Anthos, you give a document
to your Kubernetes cluster

00:31:47.270 --> 00:31:49.670
that describes the
services that you

00:31:49.670 --> 00:31:51.650
want to have up and running.

00:31:51.650 --> 00:31:55.310
And so with Cloud Run,
the fully managed version,

00:31:55.310 --> 00:31:58.760
there isn't a direct
way to [? du coup ?]

00:31:58.760 --> 00:32:01.220
ctl apply of a document.

00:32:01.220 --> 00:32:04.550
And that's because there isn't
a Kubernetes cluster actually

00:32:04.550 --> 00:32:05.210
there.

00:32:05.210 --> 00:32:08.060
There is no Kubernetes cluster
with the fully managed Cloud

00:32:08.060 --> 00:32:09.710
Run.

00:32:09.710 --> 00:32:11.810
There is-- one of
our co-workers, Amit,

00:32:11.810 --> 00:32:14.820
who created a clever
way to do this.

00:32:14.820 --> 00:32:17.840
And so it's simulating
a Kubernetes cluster

00:32:17.840 --> 00:32:20.600
but then does the, what's
needed underneath the covers

00:32:20.600 --> 00:32:23.000
to talk to Cloud
Run fully managed.

00:32:23.000 --> 00:32:26.390
But there is a way to
get ymal documents out

00:32:26.390 --> 00:32:29.270
of Cloud Run fully managed, so
that then if you are migrating

00:32:29.270 --> 00:32:32.240
from Cloud Run fully managed,
to Cloud for Anthos, or Knative,

00:32:32.240 --> 00:32:34.880
you can get the ymal document
definition that defines

00:32:34.880 --> 00:32:37.010
your Cloud Run services out.

00:32:37.010 --> 00:32:40.730
And then you can kubectl apply
those into Knative or Cloud

00:32:40.730 --> 00:32:41.690
Run for Anthos.

00:32:41.690 --> 00:32:43.580
So there is great
interoperability there

00:32:43.580 --> 00:32:44.360
between them.

00:32:44.360 --> 00:32:49.010
But because there isn't actually
a Kubernetes cluster with Cloud

00:32:49.010 --> 00:32:53.900
Run fully managed, there isn't a
direct officially supported way

00:32:53.900 --> 00:32:55.065
to apply that document.

00:32:55.065 --> 00:32:55.940
STEPHANIE WONG: Nice.

00:32:55.940 --> 00:32:57.158
OK, thank you so--

00:32:57.158 --> 00:32:58.700
JAMES WARD: At least
through kubectl.

00:32:58.700 --> 00:33:00.350
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah, Awesome.

00:33:00.350 --> 00:33:02.112
So we have a couple
more of them I'm

00:33:02.112 --> 00:33:04.320
trying to squeeze in here
with the time that we have.

00:33:04.320 --> 00:33:08.030
So @RahulG asks,
"Is Cloud Trace also

00:33:08.030 --> 00:33:11.390
available automatically
for the Cloud Run service?"

00:33:11.390 --> 00:33:12.440
Martin.

00:33:12.440 --> 00:33:14.510
MARTIN OMANDER: Yes.

00:33:14.510 --> 00:33:18.350
So yes, it has some
default stuff in there.

00:33:18.350 --> 00:33:22.130
Often I hear users
want to add more stuff,

00:33:22.130 --> 00:33:26.120
so you get some
training for free.

00:33:26.120 --> 00:33:29.750
If you want more
tracing you just

00:33:29.750 --> 00:33:33.200
pull in the OpenTelemetry
or Cloud trace libraries

00:33:33.200 --> 00:33:36.650
and then you can add more
detailed instrumentation.

00:33:36.650 --> 00:33:38.390
And just so
everybody knows, this

00:33:38.390 --> 00:33:40.460
is a really cool
thing Cloud Trace.

00:33:40.460 --> 00:33:42.380
In Cloud Trace you
can see, for example,

00:33:42.380 --> 00:33:46.250
like my slowest response times.

00:33:46.250 --> 00:33:47.660
What happens inside those?

00:33:47.660 --> 00:33:50.150
Is it the database that's
holding up the response

00:33:50.150 --> 00:33:51.980
or is it calling
to another service?

00:33:51.980 --> 00:33:53.690
And you get pretty
graphs of that

00:33:53.690 --> 00:33:56.580
and can troubleshoot and narrow
down where things go wrong.

00:33:56.580 --> 00:33:57.580
STEPHANIE WONG: Awesome.

00:33:57.580 --> 00:33:59.210
Yeah, definitely
leverage Cloud Trace.

00:33:59.210 --> 00:34:02.240
OK, so the next
question, @TheDiscoMole,

00:34:02.240 --> 00:34:06.140
which paints a very interesting
picture in my head, asks,

00:34:06.140 --> 00:34:08.360
"Can you speed up
your cold starts

00:34:08.360 --> 00:34:10.969
so choosing Cloud
Run over AWS Lambda

00:34:10.969 --> 00:34:13.340
is finally a no brainer?"

00:34:13.340 --> 00:34:15.320
Martin, I'll go to you.

00:34:15.320 --> 00:34:18.080
MARTIN OMANDER: Yeah, so there
are best practices in the Cloud

00:34:18.080 --> 00:34:21.739
Run documentation for reducing
startup times and cold start

00:34:21.739 --> 00:34:22.429
times.

00:34:22.429 --> 00:34:24.530
But then recently,
there is a feature

00:34:24.530 --> 00:34:27.889
that I use on every
single Cloud Run service

00:34:27.889 --> 00:34:29.870
I deploy nowadays when--

00:34:29.870 --> 00:34:31.790
if I'm really concerned
about cold starts.

00:34:31.790 --> 00:34:35.510
And that is a new feature
called, min instances.

00:34:35.510 --> 00:34:37.760
So when you deploy your Cloud
Run service you can say,

00:34:37.760 --> 00:34:39.260
I want there always--

00:34:39.260 --> 00:34:40.980
I don't want it to
scale down to zero,

00:34:40.980 --> 00:34:42.230
so that would be a cold start.

00:34:42.230 --> 00:34:45.980
I always want there to be
at least one server ready

00:34:45.980 --> 00:34:47.880
with my servers loaded on it.

00:34:47.880 --> 00:34:53.826
So that way, you get way fewer
cold starts, min instances.

00:34:53.826 --> 00:34:55.909
JAMES WARD: And you could
set that to be whatever.

00:34:55.909 --> 00:34:57.117
So it doesn't have to be one.

00:34:57.117 --> 00:35:00.010
If you want there to be
100 instances just waiting

00:35:00.010 --> 00:35:01.990
for requests then you
could set it to 100.

00:35:01.990 --> 00:35:04.900
You would, of course, pay
a little bit more money,

00:35:04.900 --> 00:35:07.895
but certainly an option to
set that to avoid cold starts.

00:35:07.895 --> 00:35:08.770
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah.

00:35:08.770 --> 00:35:10.228
And there are a
couple other things

00:35:10.228 --> 00:35:12.400
you can do in terms
of initialization

00:35:12.400 --> 00:35:15.340
of the container on
Cloud Run, right?

00:35:15.340 --> 00:35:17.470
So like for example,
creating a leaner service,

00:35:17.470 --> 00:35:19.090
reducing module
loading that happens

00:35:19.090 --> 00:35:22.990
if you're on Node.js or Python,
shortening your initialization

00:35:22.990 --> 00:35:24.220
in general.

00:35:24.220 --> 00:35:26.677
Anything else to add
in terms of that?

00:35:26.677 --> 00:35:28.510
MARTIN OMANDER: Yeah,
there's a good rundown

00:35:28.510 --> 00:35:31.780
in the Docs for Cloud
Run about those things

00:35:31.780 --> 00:35:33.990
that call out what you
described here, Stephanie,

00:35:33.990 --> 00:35:36.517
and a few other things.

00:35:36.517 --> 00:35:38.850
JAMES WARD: I'll point out
in the world of Java-- that's

00:35:38.850 --> 00:35:41.190
the world that I'm in,
Java, Kotlin, and Scala.

00:35:41.190 --> 00:35:45.780
There's a kind of newer tool
called GraalVM native image

00:35:45.780 --> 00:35:48.330
that allows you to take
JVM based applications

00:35:48.330 --> 00:35:50.040
and ahead of time, compile them.

00:35:50.040 --> 00:35:52.380
And I was able to take an
application that would take

00:35:52.380 --> 00:35:54.030
like five seconds to start.

00:35:54.030 --> 00:35:56.100
And with GraalVM
native image I was

00:35:56.100 --> 00:35:58.710
able to make it start in
like 100 milliseconds.

00:35:58.710 --> 00:36:00.540
And so if you're in
the world of JVM,

00:36:00.540 --> 00:36:02.010
check out GraalVM native image.

00:36:02.010 --> 00:36:03.910
It's pretty awesome
for serverless

00:36:03.910 --> 00:36:06.913
and avoiding cold starts.

00:36:06.913 --> 00:36:07.830
STEPHANIE WONG: Sweet.

00:36:07.830 --> 00:36:08.330
Cool.

00:36:08.330 --> 00:36:09.390
Check that out.

00:36:09.390 --> 00:36:12.120
OK, so I think this is our
final question that we have time

00:36:12.120 --> 00:36:15.580
for today, which is from
@ManpreetS, who asks,

00:36:15.580 --> 00:36:18.180
"It would be nice
if we could automate

00:36:18.180 --> 00:36:20.310
processes and workflows."

00:36:20.310 --> 00:36:22.330
I think he wants
that to be addressed.

00:36:22.330 --> 00:36:24.392
So, Martin, how about you first?

00:36:24.392 --> 00:36:26.850
MARTIN OMANDER: Yeah, so I
don't know exactly what Manpreet

00:36:26.850 --> 00:36:30.750
is asking for-- what Manpreet
means by automating workflows.

00:36:30.750 --> 00:36:33.570
There is a product that
just became available called

00:36:33.570 --> 00:36:34.500
Workflows.

00:36:34.500 --> 00:36:37.770
And it works great
with serverless, also

00:36:37.770 --> 00:36:38.910
full applications.

00:36:38.910 --> 00:36:41.220
What we've seen when we
started using Workflow

00:36:41.220 --> 00:36:43.380
ourselves is that
a lot of the code--

00:36:43.380 --> 00:36:49.050
or that we would write in code
like, do this, call this thing,

00:36:49.050 --> 00:36:50.250
wait if that happens.

00:36:50.250 --> 00:36:52.830
You can now write
declaratively in ymal

00:36:52.830 --> 00:36:55.440
or in JSON, which is far safer.

00:36:55.440 --> 00:36:57.420
So all those if statements
and all that stuff

00:36:57.420 --> 00:37:00.450
is declarative in
a file, that then

00:37:00.450 --> 00:37:04.440
deals with basically
translating a flowchart

00:37:04.440 --> 00:37:06.990
into calling services
in a certain order

00:37:06.990 --> 00:37:09.030
and branching and so on.

00:37:09.030 --> 00:37:09.540
Workflows.

00:37:09.540 --> 00:37:10.980
Check that out.

00:37:10.980 --> 00:37:13.170
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah,
that just came out and--

00:37:13.170 --> 00:37:14.670
is it in beta or GA now?

00:37:14.670 --> 00:37:15.960
I think it might be GA.

00:37:15.960 --> 00:37:17.605
MARTIN OMANDER:
I think so, yeah.

00:37:17.605 --> 00:37:18.480
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah.

00:37:18.480 --> 00:37:19.080
Awesome, yeah.

00:37:19.080 --> 00:37:20.850
I mean that's for
chaining together APIs

00:37:20.850 --> 00:37:23.490
and it would be great to be
used in conjunction with Cloud

00:37:23.490 --> 00:37:25.140
Run and Cloud
Functions, for example.

00:37:25.140 --> 00:37:27.420
Or anything that has an API.

00:37:27.420 --> 00:37:28.170
Great.

00:37:28.170 --> 00:37:31.200
James, you have anything
else to add here?

00:37:31.200 --> 00:37:32.290
JAMES WARD: No.

00:37:32.290 --> 00:37:32.790
Yeah.

